# Azure LVM Cleanup Script

A production-grade, idempotent Bash script for safely removing Azure-managed LVM storage from Linux virtual machines.

Unlike generic LVM removal scripts, this utility performs extensive validation before making any destructive changes, ensuring that only the intended Azure disk is cleaned up.

The script is specifically designed to mirror the provisioning model used by MyCloud:

```
1 Mount Point
      │
      ▼
1 Azure Managed Disk (LUN)
      │
      ▼
1 Partition
      │
      ▼
1 Physical Volume (PV)
      │
      ▼
1 Volume Group (VG)
      │
      ▼
1 Logical Volume (LV)
```

The cleanup process reverses the provisioning sequence safely and predictably.

---

# Features

- Idempotent execution
- Supports multiple mount points in a single run
- Uses Azure LUN as the authoritative disk identifier
- Validates mount-to-LUN mapping before cleanup
- Prevents accidental deletion of the OS disk
- UUID-based `/etc/fstab` cleanup
- Automatic `fstab` backup before modification
- Comprehensive pre-execution validation
- Dry-run mode
- Interactive confirmation before execution
- Detailed logging
- Safe handling of open file handles
- Gracefully skips already-removed resources

---

# Cleanup Workflow

For every mount/LUN pair, the script performs the following sequence:

```
Resolve Azure Disk
        │
        ▼
Validate Mapping
        │
        ▼
Unmount Filesystem
        │
        ▼
Remove /etc/fstab Entry
        │
        ▼
Remove Logical Volume
        │
        ▼
Remove Volume Group
        │
        ▼
Remove Physical Volume
        │
        ▼
Wipe Disk Signatures
```

This is intentionally the reverse of the provisioning workflow.

---

# Design Principles

The script was designed around several core principles.

## 1. Azure LUN is the Source of Truth

The script never searches disks by name.

Instead it resolves the Azure device from:

```
/dev/disk/azure/scsi1/lun<N>
```

This guarantees cleanup always targets the correct managed disk.

---

## 2. Idempotent

Running the script multiple times is safe.

If a resource has already been removed, the script simply reports it and continues.

Examples:

- LV already deleted
- VG already removed
- Mount already unmounted
- fstab entry missing

None of these are treated as failures.

---

## 3. Fail Fast

Execution stops before any destructive operation if validation fails.

Examples include:

- Invalid mount path
- Invalid LUN
- Missing Azure disk
- Wrong mount/LUN mapping
- Device resolves to OS disk
- Missing required resources

---

## 4. Safety First

The script contains multiple layers of protection before deleting anything.

It validates:

- Azure LUN
- Mount path
- LVM relationships
- PV ownership
- VG membership
- LV backing device
- Root disk protection

Only after every validation succeeds will cleanup begin.

